# Response headers for app.evnx.dev
#
# A static export has no server, so it can set no headers of its own. Every
# header below therefore has to come from the host. Cloudflare Pages and Netlify
# both read this file format, so it is not wasted if the host changes; Vercel
# would need the same values expressed in `vercel.json` instead.
#
# ─── The CSP was measured against a real build, not copied from a template ────
#
# Two of these values are load-bearing and were each confirmed by watching the
# app break without them:
#
#   * 'wasm-unsafe-eval' — WITHOUT IT THE ENTIRE CRYPTO LAYER FAILS.
#     `WebAssembly.instantiateStreaming` counts as script evaluation, so the
#     browser refuses it and the observed error is
#         "Compiling or instantiating WebAssembly module violates … because
#          'unsafe-eval' is not an allowed source of script"
#     — which reads like a bundler problem and is not one. It is narrower than
#     'unsafe-eval': it permits WebAssembly compilation and nothing else.
#
#   * 'unsafe-inline' in script-src — Next's static export emits inline
#     <script> blocks carrying the hydration bootstrap and each page's RSC
#     payload. With `script-src 'self'` alone, hydration is blocked and the page
#     renders but does nothing at all.
#
#     This is the one genuine weakness in the policy, and it is a consequence of
#     `output: "export"`: nonces need a server to mint one per request, and there
#     is no server. The alternative is hashing — this build has 11 distinct
#     inline scripts, one shared bootstrap plus one RSC payload per page — which
#     is workable but must be regenerated on every build, and a stale hash breaks
#     the app rather than degrading it. Not worth the fragility yet; revisit if
#     the number stabilises.
#
#     What limits the damage meanwhile: `default-src 'none'` means an injected
#     inline script still cannot reach any origin except the API, `connect-src`
#     names that API exactly, and no key material exists in the JS heap for it to
#     read — the keys are in the Worker's wasm memory behind opaque handles.
#
# `worker-src 'self' blob:` — the crypto Worker is served from _next/static, but
# blob: is listed because bundler worker shims fall back to it.
#
# ⚠️ Adding a third-party script, font, or analytics endpoint means adding it
# here too, and every addition widens what an injected script can reach. The
# whole point of this app is that the server cannot read your secrets; a tag
# manager on this origin could.

/*
  Content-Security-Policy: default-src 'none'; script-src 'self' 'unsafe-inline' 'wasm-unsafe-eval'; style-src 'self' 'unsafe-inline'; img-src 'self' data:; font-src 'self'; connect-src 'self' https://api.evnx.dev; worker-src 'self' blob:; manifest-src 'self'; base-uri 'none'; form-action 'none'; frame-ancestors 'none'; object-src 'none'
  X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff
  X-Frame-Options: DENY
  Referrer-Policy: no-referrer
  Cross-Origin-Opener-Policy: same-origin
  Permissions-Policy: camera=(), microphone=(), geolocation=(), payment=(), usb=()
  Strict-Transport-Security: max-age=63072000; includeSubDomains

# Hashed filenames, so these are safe to cache forever. Without this every
# visit re-downloads a 279 KB wasm module that cannot have changed.
/_next/static/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable

# HTML must revalidate, or a deploy does not reach anyone still holding a tab.
/*.html
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=0, must-revalidate
